¶
'''tittlebat''' (tit′l-bat), ''n.'' [Corrupt. for ''stickleback''.] Same as ''stickleback''.

{{smaller block|There sat the man who had . . . agitated the scientific world with the Theory of ''Tittlebats''. {{right|''Dickens'', Pickwick, i.}}}}

'''tittler†''' (tit′lėr), ''n.'' [ME. ''titeler'', ''tuteler'', ''totiler''; < ''tittle''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] A tattler; a prater.

{{smaller block|''Tituleris'' . . .<br>That bablid ffor the best.{{right|''Richard the Redeless'', iv. 57.}}
Be no ''totiler''.{{right|''MS. Bibl. Reg.'' 17 B. xvii. f. 141. (''Halliwell''.)}}}}

'''tittle-tattle''' (tit′l-tat″l), ''v. i.'' [< ''tittle''<sup>1</sup> + ''tattle''; or a varied reduplication of ''tattle''.] To talk idly; prate; gabble.

{{smaller block|You must be ''tittle-tattling'' before all our guests.{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 4. 248.}}}}

'''tittle-tattle''' (tit′l-tat″l), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''title-tatle''; < ''tittle-tattle'', ''v.''] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' Idle, trifling talk; insignificant gossip.

{{smaller block|The daily ''tittle-tattle'' of a court,<br>By common fame retail'd as office news<br>In coffee-houses, taverns, cellars, stews.{{right|''Chatterton'', Resignation.}}
A readable Life of Pitt, which would give all the facts and none of the ''tittle-tattle'', . . . is quite possible.{{right|''The Academy'', Oct. 18, 1890, p. 336.}}}}

'''2.''' An idle, trifling talker; a gossip. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Dame Polupragma, gossip ''Title-tatle'',<br>Suffers her tongue, let loose at randome, pratle<br>Of all occurrentes.{{right|''Times' Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 103.}}
Impertinent ''Tittletattles'', who have no other variety in their discourse but that of talking slower or faster.{{right|''Addison'', Tatler, No. 157.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Gossiping; gabbling. [Rare.]

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|And then at christenings and gossips feasts
A woman is not seene, the men doe all
The ''tittle-tattle'' duties. ''Brome'', Antipodes, i. 6.}}

{{smaller block|The ''tittle-tattle'' town.
{{right|''W. Combe'', Dr. Syntax's Tours, ii. 31.}}}}

'''tittle-tattler''' (tit′l-tat″lėr), ''n.'' One who circulates idle gossip; a trifling tattler. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|It was somewhat doubtful whether the ''tittle-tatler'' had improved on the usual version of the story.
{{right|''The Academy'', Jan. 29, 1889, p. 76.}}}}

'''tittle-tattling''' (tit′l-tat″ling), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''tittle-tattle'', ''v.''] The practice of dealing in idle gossip; a tattling about trifles.

{{smaller block|You are full in your ''tittle-tattlings'' of Cupid; here is Cupid, and there is Cupid. ''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, ii.}}

'''tittup, titup''' (tit′up), ''v. i.'' [< ''tit'', appar. a vague variant of ''tip''<sup>2</sup>, + ''up''.] To act or go in a gay, lively, or impatient manner; spring; prance; skip.

{{smaller block|It would be endless to notice . . . the "Dear me's" and "Oh la's" of the ''titupping'' misses.
{{right|''Scott'', St. Ronan's Well, xiii.}}}}

{{smaller block|A magnificent horse dancing, and ''tittupping'', and tossing, and performing the most graceful caracoles and gambadoes. ''Thackeray'', Philip, viii.}}

'''tittup, titup''' (tit′up), ''n.'' [< ''tittup'', ''v.''] A lively or gay movement or gait; a prancing or springing about; a canter.

{{smaller block|Citizens in Crowds, upon Pads, Hackneys, and Hunters; all upon the ''Tittup'', as if he who Rid not a Gallop was to forfeit his Horse.
{{right|Quoted in ''Ashton's'' Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, [I. 84.}}}}

