{{smaller block|But what became of this ''titubating'', this towering mountain of snow?
{{right|''Waterhouse'', Apol. for Learning, p. 29. (''Latham.'')}}}}

'''titubation''' (tit-ū-bā′shọn), ''n.'' [= F. ''titubation'' = Pg. ''titubeação'' = It. ''titubazione'', < L. ''titubatio(n-)'', a staggering, < ''titubare'', stagger: see ''titubate''.] 1. The act of stumbling or staggering; a tottering.—2. In ''med.'', restlessness; an inclination to constant change of position; fidgets.—3. The act of rocking or rolling, as a curved body on a plane.

'''titular''' (tit′ū-lär), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [= F. ''titulaire'' = Sp. Pg. ''titular'' = It. ''titolare'', < ML. *''titularis'', pertaining to a title, < L. ''titulus'', title: see ''title''.] I. ''a.'' 1. Of, pertaining to, or having a title, in any sense; existing in or by reason of title; so designated or entitled: as, ''titular'' rank, dignity, or rights; ''titular'' possession; a ''titular'' professor or incumbent of office (that is, one bearing the title, in distinction from an adjunct or a deputy).

{{smaller block|The ''titular'' Dr. Lamb is committed to the Gate-house, about causing a Westminster scholar to give himself to the devil. {{right|''Court and Times of Charles I.'', I. 305.}}}}

2. Existing in or having the title only; being such only in name; so-called; nominal; not actual: as, a ''titular'' sovereignty or bishopric; the line of ''titular'' kings of Jerusalem.

{{smaller block|I appeal to any Reader if this is not the Conditions in which these ''Titular'' Odes appear.
{{right|''Congreve'', On the Pindaric Ode.}}}}

{{smaller block|This ''titular'' sovereign of half a dozen empires, in which he did not actually possess a rood of land.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., i. 2.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
3. Receiving the name (of), or used by name, as part of a title; giving or taking title. See quotation, and ''titular church'', below.

{{smaller block|The present cardinals ''titular'' of the basilican churches of San Marco, and of the Sti. Apostoli.
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., VI. 207.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Titular abbot.''' See ''abbot.''—'''Titular bishop''', in the ''Rom. Cath. Ch.'', a bishop bearing the name of a former Christian see in which the Christian church has ceased to exist, chiefly in Mohammedan countries. This term was substituted by decree of the Propaganda, 1882, for that of "bishop in partibus infidelium," formerly in use. A titular bishop is usually assigned to episcopal duties in a country or locality where no Roman Catholic diocese exists or can be established, under the local designation of ''vicar apostolic.''—'''Titular church''', one of the parish churches of Rome, the names of which are used in the titles of cardinal priests. Compare ''title'', ''n.'', 15 (''a'').}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' 1. A person who holds a title of office, or a right of possession independently of the functions or obligations properly implied by it; in ''eccles. law'', one who may lawfully enjoy a benefice without performing its duties.—2. One whose name is used as a title; specifically, the patron saint of a church.—{{smaller|'''Titular of a church''', in the ''Rom. Cath. Ch.'', that sacred person or thing from which a church receives its title: the term is wider than ''patron'', and may comprehend the persons of the Trinity, the mysteries, or the saints, while a patron can be only a saint or an angel. ''Cath. Dict.''—'''Titulars of the tithes''', in ''Scotch eccles. law'', the titulars or lay patrons to whom the teinds or tenth part of the produce of lands, formerly claimed by the clergy, had been granted by the crown.}}

'''titularity''' (tit-ụ̄-lar′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''titular'' + ''-it-y''.] The state of being titular; use as a title of office.

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|Julius, Augustus, and Tiberius with great humility or popularity refused the name of Imperator, but their successors have challenged that title, and retained the same even in its ''titularity''. {{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., vii. 16.}}}}

'''titularly''' (tit′ū-lär-li), ''adv.'' In a titular manner; by or with regard to title; nominally.

'''titulary''' (tit′ū-lā-ri), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [= F. ''titulaire'' = It. ''titolario'', < ML. *''titularius'', pertaining to a title (cf. ''titularius'', n., a writer of titles), < L. ''titulus'', a title: see ''title'', and cf. ''titular''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Consisting in a title; bearing a title; titular.

{{smaller block|Richard Smith, ''titulary'' Bishop of Chalcedon, taking his honour from Greece, his profit from England (where he bishoped it over all the Romish Catholics), was now very busy. {{right|''Fuller'', Ch. Hist., XI. ii. 7.}}}}

'''2.''' Of or pertaining to a title; dependent upon or proceeding from a right or title.

{{smaller block|William . . . the Conquerour, howsoever he used and exercised the power of a Conquerour to reward his Normans, yet . . . mixed it with a ''Titulary'' pretence grounded upon the will and designation of Edward the Confessor. {{right|''Bacon'', Hist. Henry VII., p. 5.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.''; pl. ''titularies'' (-riz). The holder of a title; a titular incumbent or holder.

{{smaller block|The persons deputed for the celebration of these masses were neither ''titularies'' nor perpetual curates, but persons entirely conductitious. {{right|''Ayliffe'', Parergon.}}}}

'''tituled†''' (tit′ūld), ''a.'' [< L. ''titulus'', title (see ''title''), + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having or bearing a title; entitled.

'''titup, tituppy.''' See ''tittup, tittuppy''.

'''tit-warbler''' (tit′wâr″blėr), ''n.'' A bird of the subfamily ''Parinæ''. ''Swainson.''
